Day 18 of my DevOps Scenario-Based Interview Series

🔥 Scenario: Jenkins Pipeline Suddenly Becomes Slow

Imagine this:

Your Jenkins pipeline normally takes 10 minutes to complete.

But suddenly, the same pipeline starts taking 35–40 minutes.

❌ No build failure
❌ No deployment failure
✅ Pipeline eventually completes successfully

As a DevOps Engineer, what would you check?

🔍 My Troubleshooting Approach

I would troubleshoot step by step instead of immediately restarting Jenkins.

1️⃣ Check Build History

Compare the current build with previous successful builds.
Identify which stage has increased in execution time.

2️⃣ Check Jenkins Agent

* CPU and memory utilization
* Disk space and disk I/O
* Number of builds running on the agent
* Agent connectivity

3️⃣ Analyze Individual Pipeline Stages

For example:

```text
Checkout       → 1 min
Maven Build    → 25 min  ❌
Docker Build   → 3 min
Docker Push    → 2 min
Deployment     → 2 min
```

Now we know the Maven build is the bottleneck.

4️⃣ Check External Dependencies

* Maven dependency downloads
* Git repository performance
* Nexus/Artifactory availability
* Docker registry/ECR performance
* External APIs used during the build

5️⃣ Check Disk & Workspace

```bash
df -h
du -sh *
```

A full disk or unnecessarily large workspace can impact build performance.

6️⃣ Check Jenkins Logs

Look for:

* Agent connection issues
* Network timeouts
* Plugin errors
* Executor issues
* Repeated retries

7️⃣ Check Recent Changes

I would investigate whether there were recent changes to:

* Jenkinsfile
* Maven configuration
* Dockerfile
* Dependencies
* Jenkins plugins
* Agent configuration

🎯 Example Root Cause

Suppose we discover that the Maven dependency cache was deleted on the Jenkins agent.

As a result, every build is downloading dependencies again, increasing the build time significantly.

✅ Possible Fix

* Restore Maven dependency caching
* Avoid unnecessarily deleting the `.m2` repository
* Use an internal artifact repository
* Monitor pipeline execution time after the fix

💡 Interview Tip

Don't immediately say:

> "I will restart Jenkins."

A better answer is:

> "First, I would identify which pipeline stage is causing the delay, then check the Jenkins agent, system resources, network connectivity, external dependencies, logs, and recent configuration changes before applying a fix."

That shows a structured troubleshooting mindset. which is very important in real-time DevOps environments.
